t-4420.body:
I am iterating over a Vec field and trying to push into another field of the same struct in the loop body:

struct Graph { nodes: Vec<Node>, log: Vec<String> }
impl Graph {
    fn walk(&mut self) {
        for n in &self.nodes {
            self.log.push(format!("visited {}", n.id));
        }
    }
}

error[E0502]: cannot borrow `self.log` as mutable because `self.nodes` is also borrowed as immutable

I understand the words but not why this is unsafe. The two fields do not overlap. How do I write this?

replies.body:
The borrow checker reasons about whole paths through method calls, but within one function body it is field-sensitive. Your error comes from the fact that `&self.nodes` borrows through `self`, and `self.log.push` needs `&mut self.log` through the same `self`. Disjoint field borrows are allowed when both are spelled out as fields in the same scope, so this compiles:

let Graph { nodes, log } = self;
for n in nodes.iter() {
    log.push(format!("visited {}", n.id));
}

Destructuring splits the `&mut Graph` into a `&Vec<Node>` and a `&mut Vec<String>` and the checker can see they do not overlap. The version that fails is the one where the loop calls a method on self (`self.visit(n)`), because a method takes all of self and the checker cannot look inside.

t-4427.body:
function getUser() {
  return fetch('/api/me').then(r => r.json());
}
const user = getUser();
console.log(user.name); // undefined
console.log(user);      // Promise { <pending> }

The request works in the network tab. Why is `user` not the object?

replies.body:
Because `fetch` is asynchronous, `getUser` returns before the response arrives, and what it returns is a Promise that will resolve to the object later. You cannot get the value out synchronously; you can only say what to do when it arrives:

getUser().then(user => console.log(user.name));

or in an async function:

const user = await getUser();
console.log(user.name);

Once something is asynchronous, everything that depends on it is too. There is no `.unwrap()` that turns a Promise back into a plain value, which is the thing everyone looks for in the first week.

t-4441.body:
I want a 200px sidebar, a flexible content area, and a 240px right rail.

.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 200px auto 240px;
}

The middle column is only as wide as its content and the whole thing sits on the left. What is the right unit?

replies.body:
Use `1fr` instead of `auto`:

grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 240px;

`auto` sizes the track to its content; `fr` hands out the free space left over after the fixed tracks. With `1fr` there is one flexible track and it gets all of the remainder. If you also want the layout to collapse on narrow screens, add a media query that sets `grid-template-columns: 1fr` and let the three areas stack.

t-4445.body:
function first<T>(items: T[]): T { return items[0]; }
const x = first(['a', 'b']);   // string, fine
const y = first([1, 'a']);     // string | number, fine
const z = first([] as const);  // never?

And separately, this signature does not constrain anything:

function pluck<T, K>(obj: T, key: K) { return obj[key]; }
// error: Type 'K' cannot be used to index type 'T'

How do I say "K is a key of T"?

replies.body:
Add a constraint with `extends`:

function pluck<T, K extends keyof T>(obj: T, key: K): T[K] {
  return obj[key];
}

Now `K` must be one of the keys of `T`, and the return type `T[K]` is the exact property type rather than a union of all of them. Your `first([] as const)` returning `never` is correct: an empty readonly tuple has no element type, so the element type is the empty type.

t-4460.body:
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
function addThree() {
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    setCount(count + 1);
  }
}

Clicking the button adds one, not three.

replies.body:
`count` is a snapshot of the state at render time, so all three calls say "set it to 0 + 1". Use the functional updater when the new state depends on the old one:

setCount(c => c + 1);

Each updater receives the latest value, so three calls in a row produce three increments. This is the same reason a stale `count` shows up inside setTimeout callbacks and effects: closures capture the render they were created in.

Because you indexed `email` and you are filtering on `lower(email)`, which is a different expression. A btree on the raw column cannot answer a question about a function of the column. Either index the expression:

CREATE INDEX events_email_lower_idx ON events (lower(email));

or store emails lowercased and filter on the column directly, or use the `citext` type. After creating the expression index, run `ANALYZE events` and check EXPLAIN again; it should switch to an Index Scan.

t-4474.body:
type User struct {
    id    int
    name  string
}
var u User
err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(`{"id": 7, "name": "sara"}`), &u)
fmt.Println(err, u) // <nil> {0 }

No error, no data. What is wrong?

replies.body:
Your fields are unexported (lowercase), and encoding/json only touches exported fields because it uses reflection and cannot set the others. Capitalise them and add tags for the JSON names:

type User struct {
    ID   int    `json:"id"`
    Name string `json:"name"`
}

Without the tags it would still work here because the match is case-insensitive, but tags make it explicit and survive renames. To catch unknown fields at decode time, use a `json.Decoder` with `DisallowUnknownFields()`.
